WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.437
MemGPT enhances agents by enabling
the LLM to manage its own context window.

2
00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:07.907
In this lesson,
you'll learn the key ideas

3
00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:12.112
behind the MemGPT research paper:
self-editing, memory, inner thoughts,

4
00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:16.349
heartbeats, context
compilation, and virtual context.

5
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.818
All right. Let's dive in.

6
00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:20.653
So what is MemGPT?

7
00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:22.088
The MemGPT Research paper

8
00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:25.358
introduced a new way to think
about using our LLMs to build agents.

9
00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:29.262
The main way to control the behavior of an
LLM agent is by changing its input

10
00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:30.964
or its context window.

11
00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:33.867
However, it's often not so simple
to determine how to construct

12
00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.036
an optimal context window for an LLM.

13
00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:40.240
Particularly with complex
LLM agents. Context windows need to include

14
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.610
everything an agent need to know
to complete a task, including information

15
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.747
from various external data sources,
user data, prior messages,

16
00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:51.584
the results on tool calls, and previous
reasoning steps or chain of thought.

17
00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:54.487
The MemGPT research paper

18
00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:58.425
showed how to build an OS for your LLM
that manages the context window.

19
00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:03.530
In other words, an LLM OS that performs
memory management. In MemGPT

20
00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:07.067
this OS is itself also an LLM agent.

21
00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:10.136
So the memory management is done
automatically.

22
00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:13.173
There are several key ideas
behind the MemGPT research paper.

23
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.643
The first is the concept of self
editing memory.

24
00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:19.679
This refers to the ability of an LLM
to edit its own memory.

25
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.650
In many LLM applications,
the system instructions or personalization

26
00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:28.650
information of the LLM is fixed In MemGPT,
an agent can update its own instructions

27
00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:32.392
or personalization information
based on things it learns during a chat.

28
00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.464
The second key idea is inner thoughts
or inner monologue. In MemGPT,

29
00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:41.835
agents are always thinking to themselves,
even if they don't reply directly

30
00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:43.203
to a user.

31
00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:46.072
In MemGPT, agents always call tools.

32
00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:48.341
For example,
when the agent wants to communicate

33
00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:51.344
with the user,
it has to call the send message tool.

34
00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:55.115
The only time an agent doesn't call a tool
is if it's outputting inner thoughts.

35
00:01:55.982 --> 00:02:00.153
MemGPT agents are designed to run many LLM
steps off of a single user input.

36
00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:04.357
For example, if the user asks the agent
to do a complicated task,

37
00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.494
you would expect the agent to run
for many steps as it breaks down

38
00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:10.497
the different aspects of the problem
into subtasks.

39
00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:14.234
In MemGPT, agents are able to loop
through the use of heartbeats.

40
00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:18.004
Whenever an MemGPT agent calls a tool, it can add

41
00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:22.509
a special heartbeat request to any tool
which will trigger a follow-up call.

42
00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:24.444
Altogether,

43
00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:26.746
these abilities self-editing memory,

44
00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:27.714
inner thoughts,

45
00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:30.717
tool outputs, looping via heartbeats.

46
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.754
These all enable MemGPT agents
to be autonomous and self-improving.

47
00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:37.824
We call MemGPT agents autonomous

48
00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:40.827
because they can take actions
by themselves while looping.

49
00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:44.898
We call MemGPT agents self-improving
because they can edit their own long

50
00:02:44.898 --> 00:02:49.435
term memories over time.
Let's walk through an example MemGPT agent step.

51
00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:52.772
Here the agent receives a new message
from the user.

52
00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:54.307
"My name is Sarah."

53
00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:57.310
The MemGPT agent first generates
some inner thoughts.

54
00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.511
The human shared their name.

55
00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:01.014
That seems like important information
to remember.

56
00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:02.248
I agree.

57
00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:04.851
Next, you can see that
the agent calls a memory function

58
00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:07.854
to save this new fact
to its permanent memory bank.

59
00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:10.990
However, if that's the only step
we let the agent take,

60
00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:12.659
the user may become confused

61
00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:15.361
because the agent never said anything
back to the user.

62
00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.263
Here Sarah says: "Hello.

63
00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:18.731
Is anyone there?"

64
00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:20.667
To allow for multi-step reasoning.

65
00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:23.670
The MemGPT agent can use
the special heartbeat feature

66
00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:27.273
by adding a special request heartbeat
argument to the function it calls.

67
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.443
The MemGPT agent is requesting
follow-up execution.

68
00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:32.612
Basically, looping.

69
00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:33.746
By requesting a heartbeat,

70
00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:35.982
the agent can run multiple steps.

71
00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:38.918
Now it can first edit its long-term memory
and then follow up

72
00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:41.921
with the response to the user
by calling the send message function.

73
00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:44.557
Now the user won't be confused.

74
00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:47.860
All the different data that makes up
our agent is collectively called

75
00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.495
the agent state.

76
00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:52.465
When we run LLM agents in a loop
each step in the loop

77
00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:54.400
is modifying the agent state.

78
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.403
In most agent frameworks,
this agent state is simply made up

79
00:03:57.403 --> 00:04:00.873
of different Python
variables held in program memory. In MemGPT,

80
00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:02.909
this state is kept

81
00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:06.112
inside of a database
so the agent can persist over time.

82
00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:10.516
For example, you could close your Python
script and rerun the same agent

83
00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.120
and your MemGPT agent will remember everything
from the last time you ran it.

84
00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:16.990
Each time
you want to let the agent take a step,

85
00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:20.660
we have to decide how we want
to turn our agent state into a prompt

86
00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.730
that will go into the LLM.
At the end of the day,

87
00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:27.333
an LLM is just a machine
that takes tokens in and spits tokens out.

88
00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:30.403
We call the process of
going from the agent state

89
00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:33.406
to a prompt: context compilation.

90
00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:36.576
How we perform context compilation
can dramatically affect

91
00:04:36.576 --> 00:04:38.177
the behavior of the agent.

92
00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:41.614
For example, if we have more messages
than we can fit inside the prompt

93
00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:44.951
or the context window,
how should we decide what messages

94
00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:47.954
we should leave out
and what messages we should include?

95
00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:50.857
This is what MemGPT is all about.

96
00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:54.160
These are the sorts of important decisions
that an LLM OS can actually make

97
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.962
automatically for you.

98
00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:59.966
Let's break down exactly what goes into
the prompt or context window of an LLM.

99
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.171
In most popular LLM APIs,
the LLM input is divided into two sections:

100
00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:08.241
The system prompt and the chat history.

101
00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:11.577
The system prompt are the instructions
that can customize

102
00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:14.947
or change the behavior of the LLM
to make it different from the base LLM.

103
00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:18.551
In this example,
our system prompt is quite simple.

104
00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:21.688
You are helpful assistant who answers questions.

105
00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:24.023
The chat
history contains a list of messages

106
00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:27.026
between the user
and the assistant or agent.

107
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.064
You can view the job of the LLM here
as simply generating a new reply

108
00:05:31.064 --> 00:05:33.232
based on the current chat history.

109
00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:36.269
Once the LLM generates the new reply,
we can add it to the end of the chat

110
00:05:36.269 --> 00:05:37.437
history.

111
00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:40.940
In MemGPT, we create a special section
of the context window

112
00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:42.909
that we call core memory.

113
00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:45.945
Core memory is used to store
important information about the user.

114
00:05:45.978 --> 00:05:49.315
To personalize the agent.
Think about how your conversations

115
00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:52.318
with your friends are different
from your conversations with strangers.

116
00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:55.021
This is because you know information
about your friends

117
00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:58.024
that conditions or personalizes
the conversation.

118
00:05:58.424 --> 00:05:59.559
In MemGPT,

119
00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:00.593
the system prompt

120
00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:04.330
also includes information
about how to edit the core memory. So an

121
00:06:04.897 --> 00:06:07.266
MemGPT agent will see both
a special reserve section

122
00:06:07.266 --> 00:06:10.803
of the context for long-term memory,
as well as understand

123
00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:13.940
that it has the power to edit this memory
if it sees fit.

124
00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:16.042
In this simple example,

125
00:06:16.042 --> 00:06:19.746
we can see that the user corrects
an incorrect fact in the agent's memory.

126
00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:22.215
The user ask, "Who am I?"

127
00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:25.651
The agent says, "I know your name is
Charles, and that you do AI research."

128
00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:29.155
Here the user then says:
"My name is actually Sarah."

129
00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:32.291
The MemGPT agent can
then use its core memory replace

130
00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:35.495
tool to immediately correct
this incorrect fact in its core memory.

131
00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:38.631
Core memory can be customized.

132
00:06:38.631 --> 00:06:41.300
For example,
we can split it into different sections

133
00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.303
one to store information
about the user or human,

134
00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:47.173
and another section
to store information about the agent.

135
00:06:47.173 --> 00:06:50.176
You'll learn how to create a customized
memory module later in the course.

136
00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:52.745
Depending on what you want
your agent to do,

137
00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:56.516
you can make this memory module as simple
or as complex as you want it to be.

138
00:06:56.783 --> 00:06:59.385
The possibilities are truly endless.

139
00:06:59.385 --> 00:07:03.823
Core memory and MemGPT is what gives
the agents the ability to learn over time.

140
00:07:04.657 --> 00:07:07.960
Remember, core memory isn't
just another message in chat history.

141
00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:10.830
It's a special reserve section
of the context window

142
00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.833
that is always visible to the agent,
no matter what.

143
00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.103
Why do we want our agents
to have the ability to learn over time?

144
00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.406
The ability to learn over
time is part of what makes humans

145
00:07:20.406 --> 00:07:23.943
so useful as we make mistakes
or receive additional training.

146
00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:26.412
We adapt your behavior to improve.

147
00:07:26.412 --> 00:07:28.815
One reason you might want agents
with a persistent memory

148
00:07:28.815 --> 00:07:31.050
is that they're much more engaging.

149
00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.051
For example,

150
00:07:32.051 --> 00:07:35.488
imagine if a user asked a chatbot
what kind of ice cream they like most.

151
00:07:36.055 --> 00:07:39.425
Here, the chatbot replies that their
favorite ice cream flavor is vanilla.

152
00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:41.561
In most LLM chatbots,

153
00:07:41.561 --> 00:07:44.831
there's no concept of persistent memory,
so the chatbot

154
00:07:44.831 --> 00:07:47.867
will eventually forget that it said
its favorite flavor is vanilla.

155
00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:51.204
So when the user makes a reference later
to the chatbots

156
00:07:51.204 --> 00:07:54.207
or in your statement, the chatbot
will say something completely different.

157
00:07:54.707 --> 00:07:57.543
This is just a simple example
of how lack of long-term

158
00:07:57.543 --> 00:08:00.546
memory can completely break immersion
in LLM applications.

159
00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:04.417
A MemGPT agent is able to recognize
that it's stated a preference

160
00:08:04.717 --> 00:08:07.620
and commit this preference
to long-term memory.

161
00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.822
So if the user asked
the same kind of question,

162
00:08:09.822 --> 00:08:13.125
the MemGPT agent can reply
with a much more realistic response.

163
00:08:13.626 --> 00:08:16.629
So what happens when you run out of space
in the chat history?

164
00:08:16.963 --> 00:08:18.598
Context windows are finite.

165
00:08:18.598 --> 00:08:22.735
So no matter how large the context window
in the base LLM, if your conversation

166
00:08:22.735 --> 00:08:25.738
goes on for long enough,
you'll eventually run out of space.

167
00:08:26.038 --> 00:08:27.073
In MemGPT,

168
00:08:27.073 --> 00:08:30.276
when you run out of space,
we first flush or evict

169
00:08:30.276 --> 00:08:32.578
a chunk of the messages
in the chat history.

170
00:08:32.578 --> 00:08:35.147
And replace them
with the recursive summary.

171
00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:38.784
We call this summary recursive
because it summarizes all of the messages

172
00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:42.455
that were evicted, which itself may
include a previously generated summary.

173
00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:46.192
Many agent frameworks
have a similar technique of truncating

174
00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:49.495
or deleting messages from the chat history
to deal with context overflow,

175
00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:52.999
but the messages are usually permanently
deleted.

176
00:08:53.099 --> 00:08:56.202
In MemGPT, we never delete any messages.

177
00:08:56.636 --> 00:09:00.239
Instead, all of the messages
that are evicted from the context window

178
00:09:00.239 --> 00:09:03.910
get inserted into a persistent database
we call recall memory.

179
00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:08.381
By moving the old messages out of the chat
history and into recall memory.

180
00:09:08.581 --> 00:09:10.883
We can free up space in the chat history

181
00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:12.618
while making sure
that the full conversation

182
00:09:12.618 --> 00:09:15.621
history is always available to the agent
if needed.

183
00:09:16.022 --> 00:09:18.891
Similar to how core memory uses
tools to function

184
00:09:18.891 --> 00:09:21.561
recall memory also uses tools.

185
00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:24.330
If an agent wants to retrieve a message
from recall memory,

186
00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:26.265
it can use the conversation search tool,

187
00:09:26.265 --> 00:09:29.168
which will search the database
of old messages.

188
00:09:29.168 --> 00:09:32.371
Think of this like the search tool in a
chat application like Facebook Messenger.

189
00:09:32.905 --> 00:09:34.340
When the chat gets too long,

190
00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:38.411
you probably use the search tool to try
to find old messages instead of scrolling

191
00:09:38.744 --> 00:09:41.747
because there's just too many messages
to scroll through.

192
00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:44.850
In this example,
the user mentioned that "timber bit me",

193
00:09:45.184 --> 00:09:49.121
so the agent uses the search tool to try
to find old messages related to timber.

194
00:09:49.789 --> 00:09:52.892
The search tool is executed
in the database and returns the results

195
00:09:52.892 --> 00:09:55.895
into the chat history
based on the search results,

196
00:09:55.962 --> 00:09:58.965
the agent is able to infer
that timber is a dog

197
00:09:58.998 --> 00:10:02.001
and that timber had previously bit the user.

198
00:10:02.001 --> 00:10:05.171
The agent can use this information
to craft and engage in response,

199
00:10:05.571 --> 00:10:08.574
saying I can't believe your dog bit
you again.

200
00:10:09.141 --> 00:10:12.678
Core memory is also limited
in size, similar to the chat history.

201
00:10:13.312 --> 00:10:16.849
Each section of the core
memory has an associated character limit.

202
00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:18.384
Here we can see that

203
00:10:18.384 --> 00:10:21.821
both the user and agent fields
have a limit of 2000 characters.

204
00:10:22.455 --> 00:10:25.625
As a developer, you can change this limit
to whatever you want it to be.

205
00:10:25.992 --> 00:10:29.061
The fundamental constraint
is that the combined system prompt,

206
00:10:29.061 --> 00:10:33.699
core memory, summary, and chat history
must all together

207
00:10:33.699 --> 00:10:36.802
fit inside of the context
window of the base LLM that you're using.

208
00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:41.674
You might be wondering what happens if
an agent runs out of space in core memory?

209
00:10:42.608 --> 00:10:45.511
For example, here
the user expresses a new fact.

210
00:10:45.511 --> 00:10:46.579
The agent wants to save it

211
00:10:46.579 --> 00:10:49.982
to the user section of core memory,
but the section is out of space.

212
00:10:50.549 --> 00:10:51.717
Don't worry.

213
00:10:51.717 --> 00:10:55.221
Similar to how the chat history
has a second unlimited tier of storage

214
00:10:55.221 --> 00:10:56.489
called recall memory.

215
00:10:56.489 --> 00:10:59.625
Core memory also has a second tier
of unlimited storage.

216
00:10:59.625 --> 00:11:01.627
We call archival memory.

217
00:11:01.627 --> 00:11:04.897
A MemGPT agent decides
what information is most important

218
00:11:04.897 --> 00:11:08.367
to keep in core memory, and what should be
stored outside of the context window.

219
00:11:08.601 --> 00:11:10.536
Inside of archival memory.

220
00:11:10.536 --> 00:11:14.140
In this example, the agent decides
that the information is just not important

221
00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:15.841
enough to put inside of core memory,

222
00:11:15.841 --> 00:11:18.844
so it puts the information
inside of archival memory.

223
00:11:18.911 --> 00:11:21.614
You could imagine a scenario
where the agent might decide

224
00:11:21.614 --> 00:11:24.617
that the information is important enough
to put in core memory.

225
00:11:24.684 --> 00:11:28.087
In that case, the agent would first evict
information out of core memory

226
00:11:28.287 --> 00:11:30.556
by moving it into archival memory.

227
00:11:30.556 --> 00:11:34.060
Then it would add it to core memory
after it had freed up space.

228
00:11:34.694 --> 00:11:38.397
You can think of archival memory as
a general data store for the MemGPT agent.

229
00:11:38.864 --> 00:11:41.867
It's all the general information
that's not important enough to keep inside

230
00:11:41.867 --> 00:11:45.404
of core memory, which is pinned
to the context window at all times.

231
00:11:46.038 --> 00:11:48.841
Because archival memory
is a general concept,

232
00:11:48.841 --> 00:11:51.410
it means that you can use it
for many different things.

233
00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:55.848
For example, you can even use archival
memory to store code or PDF documents.

234
00:11:56.716 --> 00:12:00.219
In this example, the user is asking
a question about the company handbook.

235
00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:04.090
The company handbook is simply too large
to store and set a core memory,

236
00:12:04.724 --> 00:12:07.727
so the agent has decided to place it
instead of archival memory.

237
00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.963
To answer the user's question,
the agent will first fetch

238
00:12:10.963 --> 00:12:14.467
from archival memory. The agent gets back
a result from the handbook.

239
00:12:14.667 --> 00:12:18.137
The company handbook says that the user
can take unlimited vacation days.

240
00:12:18.738 --> 00:12:21.140
Good news, it's unlimited.

241
00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:24.176
Because archival memory
and recall memory are in external storage,

242
00:12:24.477 --> 00:12:26.879
the agent actually can't see
the contents of them

243
00:12:26.879 --> 00:12:29.982
unless it explicitly uses one of
the search tools to get the information.

244
00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:32.351
This poses a problem

245
00:12:32.351 --> 00:12:36.088
if the valuable information is stored
inside of external storage,

246
00:12:36.522 --> 00:12:39.692
but all the external storage
is outside of the context window,

247
00:12:40.092 --> 00:12:43.062
How does the agent know
where to look in the first place?

248
00:12:43.062 --> 00:12:45.898
This is where the external memory
statistics come in.

249
00:12:45.898 --> 00:12:48.734
In MemGPT,
there's a special section of the context

250
00:12:48.734 --> 00:12:51.837
window that provides statistics
about what's an external memory.

251
00:12:52.571 --> 00:12:55.141
For example, if the user asks a question

252
00:12:55.141 --> 00:12:58.277
that's not clearly defined
by the core memory or chat history,

253
00:12:58.778 --> 00:13:01.547
if the agent sees that there's
a lot of external memory,

254
00:13:01.547 --> 00:13:04.784
it will first check the external memory
to see if it has the relevant information.

255
00:13:05.551 --> 00:13:08.721
Here, the memory statistics show
that both archival and recall

256
00:13:08.854 --> 00:13:10.523
have hundreds of entries.

257
00:13:10.523 --> 00:13:13.526
So when the user quizzes the agent
about their favorite kind of dog,

258
00:13:13.592 --> 00:13:16.562
the agent first looks
for the relevant data and finds it.

259
00:13:16.896 --> 00:13:20.666
As a counter-example, if the memory
statistics show that there is no data

260
00:13:20.666 --> 00:13:23.803
inside of external storage,
then there's simply no need for the MemGPT

261
00:13:24.069 --> 00:13:27.239
agent to search the external storage,
and it can just reply directly.

262
00:13:28.107 --> 00:13:31.110
Let's summarize
what you've learned so far: In MemGPT

263
00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:33.445
there are two general tiers of memory.

264
00:13:33.445 --> 00:13:35.047
Memory that's inside of the context

265
00:13:35.047 --> 00:13:38.250
window and memory
that's not inside of the context window.

266
00:13:38.984 --> 00:13:42.421
Among the memory that's out of context,
we make sure to distinguish

267
00:13:42.421 --> 00:13:46.559
between two types of memory: recall memory,
which refers to the message

268
00:13:46.559 --> 00:13:49.962
history, and archival memory,
which is a general data store.

269
00:13:50.763 --> 00:13:52.531
Agents are comprised of agent state.

270
00:13:52.531 --> 00:13:54.433
This includes the memories of the agent,

271
00:13:54.433 --> 00:13:57.436
the tools,
and the full messages. In MemGPT,

272
00:13:58.137 --> 00:14:02.241
this agent state is stored in a database
so your agents can persist forever.

273
00:14:02.808 --> 00:14:07.146
When we run LLM inference, we have to turn
this agent state into a prompt.

274
00:14:07.513 --> 00:14:09.615
We call this step context compilation.

275
00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:12.218
Congratulations!

276
00:14:12.218 --> 00:14:15.588
You now know the techniques required
to build a basic LLM OS,

277
00:14:15.955 --> 00:14:18.691
which can give your LLM agents long-term
persistent

278
00:14:18.691 --> 00:14:21.694
memory and advanced multi-step
reasoning abilities.

279
00:14:21.861 --> 00:14:25.464
The key concepts behind MemGPT
that you've learned will enable you to

280
00:14:25.464 --> 00:14:29.335
build applications where you need agents
that can remember and learn over time.

281
00:14:29.969 --> 00:14:31.237
In the rest of this course,

282
00:14:31.237 --> 00:14:34.440
you'll learn how to take these
foundational concepts to the next level

283
00:14:34.807 --> 00:14:38.477
and build much more complex memory systems.
As well as orchestrate

284
00:14:38.477 --> 00:14:42.615
multi-agent systems, where each agent
has its own long-term memory system.

